Today, we're starting a full chapter-by-chapter breakdown of Dungeon Crawler Carl, book one. The goal here is simple. This is going to be a complete walk-through of everything that happens in the book, in order, with 
enough detail that you can follow along, remember key moments, and understand how everything builds from the very beginning. So, whether you've already read it or you just want a refresher or just want a clear 
run-through of the entire story, this video is designed to give you everything that you need. So, the book begins at 2:23 a.m. So, you already know something bad's going to happen because nothing good happens after 
2:00. Here, we meet our two protagonists, Carl and his ex-girlfriend's tortoiseshell Persian show cat, Princess Donut, the Queen Anne Chunk. It's winter in Seattle, so it's freezing, but Carl is out there in his boxers, 
leather jacket, and ex's pink Crocs trying to get Princess Donut down from a tree she had jumped into from the window. Good thing they're outside, though, because everything in the world with a roof suddenly collapses. 
Cars, houses, anything that counts as indoors instantly gets crushed, and everyone inside is immediately killed. Then, a robotic voice addresses the survivors, including Carl, letting them know that Earth has been 
seized for resource extraction because humanity evidently failed to file an appeal for its own mineral and elemental rights. And so, every interior of Earth has been crushed and raw material mined. But, don't worry. 
They offered the survivors an opportunity to reclaim the lost matter through completion of an 18-level world dungeon run by the Borant Corporation. The pitch is basically, you have 1 hour to enter, each floor has 
specific rules and set number of staircases to descend to the next floor, as well as a time limit before it collapses, and it's recommended to go and find a tutorial guild as soon as possible. So, Carl, carrying Donut 
in his whitey-tighties, doesn't have much of a choice unless he wants to freeze to death, and he heads down an ornate staircase to enter floor one of the world dungeon, where he and Princess Donut officially become 
designated crawlers and learn that they have 5 days to find a nether staircase to go down before the entire level collapses. Carl also starts seeing floating interface boxes that respond to his thoughts and realizes 
Princess Donut can see them, too, and receives a variety of achievements and rewards he can't yet open for entering the dungeon. Almost immediately, they fall for an obvious trap, which, by the way, is also an 
achievement, and get chased by a goblin-built steam machine called a goblin murderdozer. While running, Carl spots a door labeled tutorial guild, and through the door, locked by a chain, he talks to an NPC and key 
character named Mordecai. As the goblins begin to close in, Donut slips through the partially open door, and Mordecai opens it fully to let Carl in at the last moment before being run down by the murderdozer. Once 
inside the tutorial guild, which is also designated as a safe zone, Carl learns that Mordecai is a level 50 rat hooligan and a guildmaster. Princess Donut then knocks over a vase containing Mordecai's mother's ashes, 
and while Mordecai is busy sneezing and complaining about his allergies, Carl realizes he needs to take care of the two remaining goblins outside with the murderdozer before they can leave and get help. So, Carl, still 
in his boxers and pink Crocs, heads back out, tosses one goblin directly into the murderdozer's spinning blades, and jumps from the top of the machine onto and splattering the final goblin. Just as he finishes, the 
murderdozer enters a {quote} boiler breach imminent state, and Carl barely dives back into the guild hall before the whole thing explodes. Mordecai then reveals he was once a crawler himself who made it to floor 11, and 
that his actual form is a changeling, meaning his physical form randomly changes each floor to match one of the mobs also featured on that floor. Most importantly, he explains that Earth is one of many human-seeded 
worlds, and we're basically a crop that's been left to grow until it was ready for resource reclamation. Carl then gets access to his crawler menu, where he gets a first look at his stats. And Mordecai explains that the 
Borant Corporation, who is taking Earth's resources and running this dungeon, makes almost all of its money through the dungeon, which is a reality show that trillions of citizens across the syndicate are currently 
obsessed with. Carl also learns about the importance of getting a high number of viewers and followers so that he can eventually get good patrons and benefactors who might send him benefactor boxes containing high-level 
gear further on in the dungeon. Carl also learns that the dungeon is run by a syndicate-neutral observer AI that monitors everything and not to disparage the showrunners on camera, as they have the power to make his 
experience through the dungeon even more dangerous than it normally is. A voice then announces that the dungeon is officially sealed, and out of the billions on Earth, only 13 million crawlers made it inside, and that 
number is already down to under 10 million. Mordecai also lets Carl know that only a handful of people ever made it past floor 10, and the deepest anybody has ever delved in history was floor 13. Now, Mordecai then 
activates Carl's inventory, which has, for this season, been upgraded to an unlimited storage system, mostly because the showrunners realized that watching crawlers slowly die of disease and starvation didn't really 
make for compelling reality TV. Carl finally gets to see his massive backlog of achievements, including rewards for killing mobs with his bare hands and bare feet. He also finds out that he's officially reached level 
two and has gained a level four pugilism skill, which increases his bare-knuckle damage by 100%. Carl opens his loot boxes in hope for a pair of pants, but of course, he doesn't get any. But, he does get quite a bit of 
decent loot, including a goblin pass that brands his arm. Carl also receives an enhanced pet Donut snatches and then immediately melts into a wet mess of flesh as her body begins to rebuild itself into something new. As 
they wait for Donut to emerge from her transformation, Carl starts digging into the rest of his loot. He equips a toad cudgel, realizes the goblin pass he pulled isn't a physical arm, but a permanent tattoo that's 
branded onto his arm, and this brand allows him safe passage through goblin-controlled territories, though fairy-class creatures will now deal 20% more damage against him. Carl also puts on the enchanted night gaunt 
cloak of stoutness, which increases his constitution, his resistance to poison and ice attacks, and adds anti-piercing resistance to his armor. He also puts on an enchanted troll skin shirt that provides a massive buff 
to his regeneration skill, allowing Carl to heal back to full health in as little as 2 minutes. He also reluctantly puts on the enchanted toe ring of the splatter skunk, which boosts his strength and his powerful 
strength skill, where each level multiplies your damage when you're not wielding a weapon. Now, Carl also discovers that the dungeon's AI tracks every obscure proficiency from his entire life, including a level eight 
skill in the 1981 arcade version of Frogger, and then, for the first time, he hears Donut's voice. Donut eventually emerges from her transformation looking mostly the same as before, though slightly bigger, and of 
course, she can now speak perfect English and insists on being addressed by her full formal title, GC BWRNW Princess Donut, the Queen Anne Chunk. Donut is now an official crawler with an extremely high charisma stat of 
25 and an enhanced growth buff that grants her an automatic plus one to strength and intelligence and plus two to charisma every time she levels. Donut opens her own loot boxes, where she gets the magic missile spell 
and the enchanted crown of the sepsis [ __ ] which Donut puts on before reading what it does, which is it'll give her attacks a chance to inflict the sepsis debuff to enemies, though it also permanently places the 
wearer into the royal line of succession for the blood sultanate on the ninth floor. This means if they ever make it that deep, she will need to kill the sultan and all other members of the royal family before being 
able to descend to the 10th floor. Now, because Donut now possesses the highest combined stat total in their group, the system officially designates her as the party leader, and she renames her party the royal court of 
Princess Donut and changes Carl's title to royal bodyguard and her title to grand champion best in dungeon. Carl and Donut leave the safety of the tutorial guild, and they encounter a level three bad llama that spits 
baseball-sized balls of lava. Donut starts to blast it with magic missiles from her eyes, while Carl lands the killing blow with a series of punches to its glowing throat. Carl's hand is shattered in the process, but 
thanks to his high regeneration skill, he's healed and back to full health within a minute. They loot the corpse and find some llama steaks and a baggy of trailer park grade meth. They continue through the floor, 
fighting off swarms of giant rats and cockroaches, and after reaching level three, the system grants Carl two new skills: foot soldier, which increases his damage when kicking, and smush, which increases the amount of 
pressure Carl can apply with his bare foot by 10% per level. Upon entering their first safe room, Carl and Donut meet Tally, a level 63 bobcat protector, who are these gnome-like little creatures that handle everything 
in safe rooms from cleaning toilets to preparing food. Now, while inside, Carl notices a horrifying update to the global crawler count on the room screens. The number of survivors has plummeted from 10 million down to 
just over four. Donut opens a gold magic item box and receives the talisman of slate butterfly, a charm that boosts her intelligence, makes winged fairies no longer automatically hostile towards her, and adds plus four 
to a new light on your feet skill, which allows her to pounce ridiculously high. After a 10-hour rest in the safe room, they head back out into the tunnels to farm experience by killing scatterer blood guardians, which 
are essentially giant cockroaches where Carl reaches level five and sees his pugilism and smush skills hit level five as well. Then Carl and Donut discover a door labeled do not enter in Spanish and Carl decides to open 
it and toss in his recently acquired goblin dynamite to hopefully kill whatever's hiding inside. The dynamite works, killing most of the bugs instantly and earning Carl several skill upgrades, including explosive 
handling and a gold crowd control box for killing over 10 mobs at once. It also nearly kills Carl, who gets blown back down the hall and hits danger low health before his regeneration kicks in. Now inside the nest, the 
door magically seals behind them and the boss battle music starts playing. They've accidentally triggered a neighborhood boss fight called the hoarder, a level seven boss who is a 15-ft tall woman who is clearly just a 
regular person when the collapse happened, now fused with the dungeon's garbage and driven insane. She's terrified, speaks in Spanish, and begs them not to hurt her, and says she didn't want her daughter to get sick, 
and asks them to send a message to Jesus. Carl has to kill her anyway. He loops a metal chain leash he finds in the garbage around her throat and strangles her while Donut runs interference with magic missiles, and Carl 
fights off waves of scatterers with his bare hands. He apologizes to her right before he finishes her off. The remaining cockroaches drop dead, the door explodes open, and the winner is announced as the royal court of 
princess Donut. Now after looting a neighborhood map from the hoarder's corpse, that allows them to see everything in that particular area on their mini-map, Carl and Donut notice an X representing a dead crawler nearby 
named Rebecca W. They head to a nearby tutorial guild where Mordecai explains more about the dungeon, revealing that there are six tiers of bosses, neighborhood, borough, city, province, country, and floor, and that 
some of them, like the hoarder, are actually recycled humans from previous dungeon seasons. Carl and Donut go ahead and open their loot boxes with Donut learning a new torch spell, which allows her to make a ball of 
light that follows them around. Carl gets a level three iron punch skill, which allows him to wear gauntlets to protect his hands without losing his bare knuckles bonus. He also receives enchanted spiked knee pads of 
the shade null riot forces, which adds 10% damage reflect and cancels momentum-based attacks, and Carl receives a tome of wis armor, which he holds onto to maybe use in the future when he has more mana, as the spell 
temporarily negates 75% incoming damage from magic-based attacks and immunity to mind control effects. Before they head back out, Mordecai mentions that the syndicate sometimes pulls crawlers out of the game for special 
on-camera interviews. Carl and Donut head back out to check on the corpse of Rebecca W. that they saw on the mini-map earlier. They find her naked with only an apple core left in her inventory and discover that she had 
been shot in the chest and killed by a crawler named Frank Q. They head back to the safe room to watch the premiere of Dungeon Crawler World Earth. The crawler count is now down to around 4 million, and the premiere is 
mostly propaganda, cherry-picked footage making Earth look like a hellhole to justify the mining, followed by 40 minutes of crawlers dying. They do show a group of 10 crawlers who took down a borough boss, the first one 
defeated, and a 13-year-old girl named Lucia Mar, who cleared mobs with two Rottweilers named CC and Gustavo III. Carl and Donut get zero screen time despite killing the hoarder, which Donut takes as a personal affront. 
The announcement that follows patches several game rules. Bathrooms can no longer be shared. You can't kill mobs by pulling them into your inventory, and going down the stair early puts you in stasis with no viewer 
access until the floor above collapses. The staircases to floor two are now open. With three and a half days left and 75,000 staircases distributed randomly across a floor the size of Earth's landmass, Carl realizes 
that finding one is going to be extremely hard and that they need to cover a lot of ground. So Carl and Donut head back out into the goblin neighborhood. Carl uses his goblin pass tattoo to get through without a fight, 
but they run into a new mob type called slimes, which nearly dissolve Carl's hand and arm when he tries to punch one. They give slimes a wide berth from here on out. They spot another neighborhood boss lair, a massive 
goblin workshop filled with about 25 engineers and two level seven goblin shamankas, and six parked murderdozers. One of the goblin shamankas is a heavily pierced character named Rory, who has a pineapple-topped wooden 
staff and specializes in English magic. Instead of fighting, Donut uses her 25 charisma stat to charm Rory, literally sitting in her lap and beginning to purr until the goblin breaks down crying. Carl negotiates. They 
trade their bags of trailer park grade meth they looted from the bad llama for a goblin chopper chopper, a steam-powered two-wheeler with a sidecar. Carl gets in the goblin chopper chopper, loaded up with coal and 
stuffed with as much dynamite grenades and goblin fun powder from the workshop as he could grab. Then Donut reveals she told the goblins the meth came from the llamas in the next neighborhood over, which immediately has 
triggered a full-scale meth war between the goblin bombards and the llamas. Four copper choppers and several murderdozers roll out screaming. And now with the workshop empty, Carl wants to loot everything and then go 
back through the door at the back to fight the goblin chieftain for another boss box. Carl loads the workshop cart with black powder, nut bolts, and a hobgoblin detonator, a remotely triggered C4-like explosive, topping 
it with shrapnel, and has Donut open the door while he shoves it into the boss room. After Carl kicks the cart in, they run and detonate it from a safe distance, but just as Carl hits the trigger, Donut does mention 
that there were also a lot of babies in there. The shockwave knocks them both over, but they're unhurt. The workshop is obliterated, and the coal has ignited, blocking the boss chamber with a wall of fire, no looting 
the boss room. Carl feels sick about the babies and can't bring himself to open his achievement notifications. Both of them hit level seven, and they flee north before the other goblins return with Donut cheerfully 
noting that she likes it when Carl blows stuff up. They find a new safe room, a dingy DMV-style waiting area where the crawler count is now down to around 3 and 1/2 million. The only amenity is one free experience 
cookie per crawler per day, which Donut eats instantly and also tries to steal Carl's. They open their loot. Carl gets a war criminal and you monster achievement for killing all the babies, and his combat skills all hit 
level five, and he unlocks an IED skill level three, which increases improvised explosive damage by 10% per level. He also gets a new demolitions workshop menu, which is which is only accessible at a sapper's table, 
which he can get in a safe room store. Now Donut gets her first piece of armor, an enchanted face scale quadruped crupper of the fleet, a scale armor skirt covering her back half, enhancing her with plus two dexterity, 
and a desperado pass tattoo that goes on her back that she is absolutely furious about. She also gets a tome of minion army that she can't quite cast yet. Carl gets the constitution ring and a skill potion, which gives 
him the determined value skill. Carl tries to sleep, but can't stop thinking about the goblin babies that he killed, who were placed there on purpose, existing only to die. Donut curls up on his neck and purrs until 
they both fall asleep. Carl wakes up later to the Dungeon Crawler Recap show starting and immediately spots Frank Q's blue dot on the women's bathroom of their safe room. They confront him in the hall. Frank is level 
eight, armed with a battle axe and throwing knives, and has three crawler killer skulls next to his name. Frank explains that he didn't have a choice killing Rebecca W. She was the boss of a human trafficking operation 
he and his partner had been investigating for ICE. Carl doesn't buy his story at all, but does trade him some blood-stained women's pants for more information on the area. Then Frank's partner, level nine Maggie Mae, 
who has five crawler killer skulls, ambushes them from behind. They barely make it out and escape. As they run away, Carl slips a lit smoke bomb into a nearby rat's inventory as a trap just in case they decide to loot 
it, and he and Donut continue running away. Carl and Donut jump on the goblin chopper chopper and ride east for hours, seeing almost nothing. They stop once to fight off a group of skath thugs, which are these bipedal 
raccoon-headed monsters, and after a few hours of riding, they spot fresh graffiti on a wall that says two crossroads east, three south, stairs plus people. They follow it and find a stairwell to floor two on their 
mini-map, sitting at the center of a massive circular structure. A warning on the ground tells them not to go deeper into the spiral or they'll activate the borough boss. Before they can decide what to do, something 
enormous rushes past them through the inner hallway, pink, fleshy, the size of the entire corridor, rolling like a ball. Whatever it is, it's very, very big. They follow the graffiti to a large encampment of about 38 
people, mostly elderly residents from a care facility in Wenatchee led by two brothers, Brandon and Chris, both level six. Brandon had evacuated 250 residents when the collapse hit, herding them into the dungeon 
entrance, though most didn't make it. A homeless woman named Agatha was the first to voluntarily go down, cackling the whole way, and the staircase had transformed into a ramp for her. Now the group also includes 
Yolanda Martinez and Imani C, who is a level 10 crawler with 12 crawler killer skulls next to her name. Carl then learns that her kill count was the direct result of having to kill 12 of her former patients. Now, they 
found the staircase, but they can't quite get to it. That massive burrow boss is still circulating the spiral maze between them and it, and when everybody enters, the entry gate closes and traps anybody who gets in. 
Now, Carl proposes teaming up to kill the boss to help get everybody down the stairs. Afterwards, his goal is to spend the remaining time grinding and leveling up. Imani speaks for the first time to ask how they're 
going to do that, and Carl grins telling them he has an idea. But first, Carl wants to cover up the signs pointing to the encampment to protect them from other crawlers, including potentially Frank and Maggie. They 
spend the next couple of hours grinding through a new neighborhood fighting troglodyte pygmies, these fast and venomous 3-ft lizard creatures, as well as level three bashers, and level four virtuoso versions who can 
also shoot their tongues like a whip to inflict poison. Luckily, Carl's cloak cancels out poison status out. Donut's magic missile levels to level five, letting her choose how many mana points to put into each cast. And 
then they find the troglodyte neighborhood boss room, a glass-doored 24-hour gym, and step inside. The boss is named the Juicer, a level nine neighborhood boss, a troglodyte fused with a roided out a bodybuilder welding 
flaming free weights. Carl uses his confusing fog scrolls to blind the room, takes out the virtuosos while Donut peppers the boss with missiles, and Carl beats the Juicer to death with a 250-lb barbell. It doesn't go 
too well, though, and just as the boss is about to finish Carl off, Donut launches herself onto his back and bites through a vein in his neck killing it, but also takes a glancing blow that sends her flying into the 
wall and leaves her in a bloody heap with a broken leg and barely a sliver of health remaining. Carl tries to use his heal critter scroll on her, but gets an error as apparently it doesn't work on other crawlers. He 
can't give her a healing potion either without risking poisoning her due to her mana cooldown, so he has to wait and watch her health bar crawl upward on its own. Donut dramatically announces she is dying and asks Carl 
to tell Beatrice that she fought bravely, and then she takes one of her own health potions and immediately perks up. They head back out to the tutorial guild to debrief with Mordecai, where he reviews the fight and 
gives Carl two key pieces of advice. Having the highest intelligence party member read scrolls to maximize its duration, and to always look for a boss's vulnerability as there are always clues. Mordecai also warns that 
facing a burrow boss with only six crawlers is madness and suggests finding another set of stairs instead. He confirms he can't tell them what the boss is ahead of time without it counting as cheating, but hints that it 
has a weakness just as the llama did and just as the Juicer did. They go ahead and open their loot. The standout items here are the enchanted war gauntlet of the exalted ghoul, a wrist bracer that transforms a spiked 
orcish steel gauntlet when Carl makes a fist, giving him plus three strength, plus one dexterity, and plus two levels to iron punch with a 2% stun chance. And he receives a hundred enchanted shuriken of bloodlust. These 
are these throwing stars that deal 8% compounding damage against a specific monster type of your choice. Now, Mordecai also lets them know that there are no real gods in the dungeon. They're just mostly entities that 
pay for the privilege of being worshipped. Carl files that little nugget away. Now, with six and a half hours left on the clock, Carl leads the group into the burrow boss spiral. The boss is the ball of swine, a level 
15 burrow boss, a massive rolling ball of compressed tuskling aristocrats, flesh, sequined fabric, and tusks that crushes everything in its path. Now, Carl's plan, which he'd spent hours practicing with Brandon and the 
others, is to build a portable modular fortress called the speed bump out of goblin workshop tables, weight equipment, and barbell braces, wedging it between the floor and ceiling to trap the ball of swine. They also 
spike the wooden ramp with the ninja shurikens to deal compounding damage each time the ball rolls over it. Agatha, the crazy lady who always walks around with her shopping cart, sits in the middle of the structure 
holding the center pole in place while Carl grabs her shopping cart and holds it in his inventory temporarily. The plan pretty much works. The ball gets wedged. They stab through the lattice ceiling into the tusklings 
inside until the ball eventually pops where it releases dozens of dazed weaponless tuskling knights into the now open arena. Carl, Donut, Brandon, Chris, and Imani slaughter them all while they're disoriented. The 
internal walls then retract revealing the staircase at the center. Everyone has survived relatively unscathed. Both Carl and Donut hit level 10, and the boss drops a burrow field guide which populates the surrounding 
area on the mini-map with mob types. Agatha has already headed down the ramp on her own, and Carl decides to help get Brandon's group safely to floor two carving them a path to the nearest safe room, and then continuing 
on separately. Brandon agrees and thanks him. Carl smokes his last cigarette, heads down the ramp with Donut, and they now descend to floor two, where they found themselves in a velvet-lined room on what appears to be a 
boat. Before they can process anything, a door opens and a non-Earth human woman named Lexis introduces herself as an associate producer for Dungeon Crawler After Hours with Odette. Apparently, they've been selected for 
an on-air interview. Donut squeals with pleasure. Carl and Donut are then prepared for their interview on Dungeon Crawler After Hours with Odette, the most popular private program in the galaxy. The host, Odette, turns 
out to be a naked crab tar wearing a bug mask. So, a lower body of a king crab, upper body of a heavily surgically enhanced human woman, and a triangular bug head with giant compound eyes. Donut immediately takes over 
introducing herself to the audience, dropping her full title, and telling them GC stands for Grand Champion. Carl watches in horror as Donut completely steals the show, charming the entire alien audience while Carl just 
sits there covered in blood with no pants. And here, Odette reveals clips featuring Carl's famous catchphrase, "God dammit, Donut." And Donut ends the interview by promising the audience they're going to kill everything 
they face, but with style. The crowd is still screaming as they're ushered off the stage. Now, once the audience disappears, Odette drops the act entirely. She removes her bug mask helmet, her fake boobs, and her crab 
body to reveal that she's a legless human woman in a magic school wheelchair who lost her legs on floor 11 during her own crawl. She reveals that she was Mordecai's trainer, which is how she found Carl and Donut. She 
gives them several key pieces of advice. Don't call Kuwa Teine Borant on camera. Donut should not change her race on floor three because they want to keep their audience happy. And most importantly, Mordecai is on his 
last crawl and won't be freed from his indentured ship contract unless one of his crawlers makes it to at least floor four. Carl is annoyed by this, but believes Odette's motivation is fairly straightforward. The longer 
they stay alive, the more money she makes. Now, Carl and Donut finally arrive on floor two. The stairwell to floor three is already visible on the mini-map in the northeast corner. Now, floor two looks similar to floor 
one, but with cinder block walls, and they immediately find a corpse, a level two brindle grub killed by Agatha, confirming that she has made it down ahead of them. The new dominant mob on floor two is this brindle 
grub, which are these large fat worms that have replaced rats as the janitor class. They also run into level five danger dingos, these giant mastiff-sized dogs with heavy metal corpse paint and cobalt riders, before 
finding a safe room and opening their loot. The standout items here are a cheat code potion with a 17-minute timer that permanently increases a random combat stat by three, so Carl drinks it immediately and his pugilism 
skill hits level 10. And he also receives the enchanted pedicure kit of the sylph, which gives plus 15% barefoot damage, plus three levels to smash, unbreakable feet, and a five-second warning before triggering traps. 
Donut gets a spellbook from her boss box, second chance, a necromancy skill that raises a dead monster to fight for her for one minute per spell level. Donut is immediately obsessed with the idea of raising zombie 
dingos. Now, the floor one collapse happens as the next episode of Dungeon Crawler World premieres. Chris speaks for only the second time so far to note that Agatha was edited out of the burrow boss fight footage, which 
is very strange. The dungeon announces a new patronage system. Crawlers are now limited to three patrons. Spots go to auction at the start of each floor starting with floor three, and the better your social numbers, the 
better your loot. It also warns that any crawler caught urinating outside a bathroom will have a rage elemental from floor 13 summoned to kill them and their entire party. Carl convinces Brandon to head to floor three 
early while he scouts ahead and clears the path. Brandon agrees, and Carl promises to find him a safe route. And the dominant mob that they find on this quadrant they are now in is the clerchin, troll-like fairy cousins 
of leprechauns armed with slingshots, and being a fairy class mob, that means they deal 20% more damage to Carl due to his goblin pass. They also inflict the taint debuff, which prevents any healing while active. Carl 
gets hit with it once, and it generally scares him. The clerchins are running a moonshine pyramid scheme called Rev Up, and they're hoarding jugs of the stuff. Carl figures out that the moonshine is 180 proof, highly 
flammable, and makes perfect Molotov cocktails. He mixes the jug with goblin oil and a torch to create what the game labels Carl's Juggaboom, an incendiary device that burns for 15 seconds per level of his incendiary 
device handling skill. Donut uses his new use skill to raise dead clerchins as zombies while Carl practices. When they reach the boss room of the area, they find a large workshop filled with a higher tier of the pyramid 
scheme hierarchy called Laminack Rev Up Consultant Elites who are immune to both health seeping attacks. The room also has two small fat fairies guarding a doorway, but Carl thinks up a plan. The plan is to have Donut 
use her puddle jumper skill to teleport near and charm the two fairy guards and pied piper the entire mob of Claracans out into the hallway where Carl can firebomb them with the jugs of boom. It mostly works. Carl 
torches about 40 of the monsters at once, but one fairy escapes to the ceiling and starts shooting magic missiles at Carl. He takes some serious damage before Donut leaps up and snaps its neck. Carl decides to skip the 
boss room for now as the taint debuff makes a direct fight too risky without immunity. So, instead Carl discovers these bicycle powered blenders in the filling room and a Rev Up Immunity Smoothie recipe that would grant 
him taint immunity. But, before he can finish making the immunity smoothies, the boss room door blast open and pink tentacles at least 15 ft long covered in screaming human mouths reach into the room. The boss is a 
Claracan clone, a level 10 neighborhood boss. Carl does manage to blend up a Rev Up Immunity Smoothie mid-fight using the bicycle blender and a dead Laminack fairy corpse. It tastes like drinking from a diseased skunk, 
but grants immunity to the taint and all communicable diseases. Though, it also inflicts the buzz debuff leaving both Carl and Donut drunk mid-combat. The fight gets increasingly desperate as more tentacles break 
through the walls and more Claracans flood in. Carl sends a zombie Claracan holding a lit jug of moonshine and a stick of dynamite shoved down its overalls into the boss chamber and then shelters behind a metal alchemy 
table pulled from his inventory and wraps himself around Donut as the explosion goes off. Carl barely survives. His health goes down to about 6% and still dropping while Donut drags him out by his cloak and uses a 
healing scroll on him. They go back into room with the boss. The Claracan ends up being this massive immobile octopus creature and Carl grabs the neighborhood map and they head back to the tutorial guild to find 
Mordecai who has on the second floor now shape-shifted into a Bugaboo, which is a 7-ft hairless creature with owl-ish eyes and comically skinny legs. Carl's IED and explosive skills has hit level seven from creating the 
chug-a-boom earning him a dungeonpreneur achievement where he receives a gold coin every time another crawler kills something with his invention. From the boss box he gets enchanted big boy boxers, which are self-sizing 
underwear with plus two constitution and a protective spell shell that creates a 4.5 m radius physical shield for 20 seconds that can be used once every 30 hours. Then, all of a sudden a Cutine appears in the room. The 
Cutine's name is Zev, who is a Burnt Corporation dungeon admin who's been assigned as Carl and Donut's PR agent due to their exploding popularity. She informs them that they're now locked into Odette's show for one 
interview per floor plus additional appearances that she'll arrange. Carl pushes back hard telling her that they're not willing participants and won't perform on a schedule. Zev drops her corporate act and admits that 
she's just as much of a pawn in this as they are. She's terrified of her own government and generally wants them to survive because the better they do, the better she does. Mordecai pulls Carl aside to inform him that 
Burnt is being forced by an ultra-nationalist political party called the Bloom to end the game as quickly as possible. The Cutine government is bankrupt and needs the money fast and this means the dungeon is being 
deliberately accelerated and made more dangerous, so Carl should try to be careful not to upset the Bloom. The Cutine Zev leaves and the door to the safe room opens to dump a waist-high pile of grubs into the room. The 
grubs are being spawned because so many mobs have been killed. Every mob destroyed spawns up to 15 more grubs and they've already hit the 5,000 per quadrant cap meaning 75,000 grubs are now converging on the entire 
neighborhood. Carl radios Brandon to get moving toward the stairs immediately. Brandon's group arrives with 39 residents riding in linked cars pulled by Imani and Chris. They firebomb a path through the grub infestation 
and escort the train toward the stairs. While mid-escort, Zev messages Carl through his interface complimentary the elderly rescue storyline and telling him it's playing great with viewers. Right as one of the elderly 
residents named Jack stands up from his wheelchair, drops his pants and starts to urinate directly on the wall. Jack urinating on the wall leads to a level 93 rage elemental being summoned, nearly indestructible and 
will only dissipate after claiming 666 souls. It materializes in black and purple smoke with six legs, obsidian claws, and a badger skull with burning red eyes. Donut immediately activates confusing fog while Yolanda 
shoots at it, though her arrows do nothing. The elemental shreds through the fog and kills Yolanda and Randall instantly before Carl can even react. He smashes down protective shell just in time, the force field 
bouncing the elemental back holding it off long enough for everybody to try and get to the safe room. The elemental uses an ability to reverse gravity mid-escape sending the last train car flying into the ceiling and 
slamming Miss McGibbons upside down into the hallway roof. Carl catches her and gets everybody through the safe door while throwing a boom jug at the elemental before diving in himself missing its claws by millimeters. 
The door holds and the elemental screams as it batters against the outside. Carl and Donut receive two achievements from the rage elemental fight, one for surviving a reverse gravity spell and a platinum lucky bastard 
box containing scratch tickets. Carl's ticket is fireball or custard. Each scratch giving him a 50/50 chance of casting either a level 15 fireball or dropping a strawberry custard on the nearest red dot. Then, the 
dungeon announces a new rule. Safe rooms will now close 1 hour before floor collapse teleporting anyone inside to just outside the area. Carl's new plan centers around the mother of all bombs, the MOAB, a bomb launcher 
built from chopper parts that they can use to fire different types of explosives down the hallway. They use it to carpet bomb a path to the stairwell using boom jugs, shredder bombs filled with gunpowder and trapnel, as 
well as Imani's unbreakable magical chain to tangle the elemental's legs. The run is chaotic. The grubs are everywhere including a newly hatched cow-tailed brindle grubs. They make it to the stairwell and remember what 
Rory the goblin shamanka had told them earlier that any NPCs attempting to descend a stairwell early would immediately get them killed. With 2 seconds before the elemental hits them, Carl drops an oh [ __ ] baby bomb 
into the stairwell hole. Donut casts puddle jumper to teleport them and the elemental falls inside the stairwell instantly killing it. Carl then radios Brandon telling him that the path is clear and to get everyone to 
the stairs. Brandon's group makes it to the stairs and Carl and Donut say their goodbyes. Carl and Donut then head to the Kobold quadrant killing more dingo riding Kobolds and clearing toward a safe room that turns out 
to be an industrial kitchen with a working freezer and some free mana toast. Carl receives a gold sappers box containing a sappers table and 10 proximity triggers, trap building items that can be attached to any 
non-static trap with customizable activation conditions. Before they can rest, a new admin named Mukta teleports them to a cramped rental trailer for a scheduled interview on a show called Death Watch Extreme Dungeon 
Mayhem hosted by the Maestro. The Maestro is a massive muscular orc who runs a cruel survivor game show for a crowd of screaming preteen boys, the audience always chanting glorp glorp at everything. The other guests are 
Lee Jun and Zeng, two warehouse workers from the same group as Rebecca W who the Maestro has pulled from a deadly situation using a time freeze spell. Carl immediately tries to leave, but admin Mukta forces him back to 
his seat under threat of acceleration. The Maestro reveals Carl and Donut as surprise VIP guests to the audience and mocks them for getting no experience from helping the elderly group and then threatens to transfer 
Carl alone into Lee Jun and Zeng's deadly fight. Carl calls his bluff pointing out that the show runs on a shoestring budget and can't afford to send their highest value guest into danger and he's right. The Maestro 
backs down and decides to send Lee Jun and Zeng back instead. Carl quickly gives them some advice to help them make it out alive. The Maestro reveals that the two other mystery VIP guests are none other than Frank Q and 
Maggie Mae. The video recap reveals Frank's entire backstory was a lie. They were never ice agents. They were actually part of Rebecca W's trafficking operation working with her until they turned on the group and shot 
everyone. They also have a daughter, Evette, who never participated in the killings, but when Carl's dynamite that he had left in the rat's inventory as a trap went off, it had injured Evette severely and her mom Maggie 
is shown killing her. Carl confronts Maggie directly about killing her own daughter for what he believes is just experience points and Maggie gets furious and lunges at him with a glowing dagger, which passes harmlessly 
through him since they aren't really in the same room. As parting gifts, the Maestro gives Carl a box containing Bates zip-up tactical boots with socks, a deliberate troll since Carl's bare feet skills would be useless 
if he wore them. Carl smiles and thanks him sincerely to Maestro's frustration. Maggie Mae receives a legendary skill potion that upgrades their fine crawler skill to level 15 meaning they can now locate Carl and Donut 
wherever they go. Back in the safe room, Zev returns. She'd been suspended by the system AI for warning Carl about the rage elemental, but her record was cleared when it was confirmed Carl had spotted Jack urinating at 
the same time. She warns Carl that she has to be extremely careful from now on. Zev also gives them a debrief, letting them know the Maestro is the prince of the Skull Empire, one of the largest syndicant governments, 
and his show is owned by his older brother, the Crown Prince Stalwart. Borant is not allowed to discriminate against syndicant production companies, so they had no choice but to allow the interview. More pressingly, 
somebody has created a high-quality illicit fan fiction video called the Snick featuring Carl and the Maestro, which has gone massively viral, which both pisses off the Skull Empire and has also doubled Carl's 
appearance fee. Carl and Donut then leave to follow heavy metal music to the Kobold boss's chamber, a giant kennel with caged danger dingos. Carl remembers Mordecai's advice and stops. He then proceeds to feed all 14 
dingos biscuits from Donut's inventories until their dots turn white. The boss doors don't open before he's ready. He throws his boom jug anyway, vaporizing most of the 40 Kobolds. The boss is Ralph, a level 11 frenzy 
gerbil. The intro explains that gerbils were the real black death culprit, and Ralph wants to finish the job. One of the surviving Kobolds opens all of the cages, but the now friendly dingos then proceed to tear the 
Kobolds apart. Ralph is eventually finally finished when Carl reluctantly crushes him under his bare foot, paying what Donut calls the daddy tax. The dungeon groans after Ralph dies. The 13 dingos scatter into the 
hallways and wipe out the newly spawned brittle grubs on their way through, and Carl loots the Kobolds and finds a master pen key, which opens a cage of their choice in the pet reward room. In the pet reward room, there 
are 10 cages: a loud parrot called the juvenile riven wing, a green slime, a rat, a meatball with a mohawk called a tummy aker, a white-dotted brittle grub, and others. Donut chooses the last cage, which is labeled as a 
mongoliosis, a tiny chicken-looking thing with pink feathers and a mouthful of sharp teeth. Donut immediately names it [ __ ] Mordecai explains to them the bonding rules. Mongo's dot has to turn white before they can 
bring him to floor three, or he will teleport away the moment they descend. Pet class mobs start at level one and stay there until bonded. The trick they figure out is that [ __ ] is a pack hunter, so he needs to be 
taught to kill alongside them, not by himself. They find lone brittle grubs and let him hunt with Donut, whom he eventually bonds with, and then Donut changes his title to royal steed. Mordecai briefs them on floor 
three, where they'll be able to select a race, class, and distribute their stats. With 6 hours left, Carl opens his inventory history tab for the first time and finds three items he doesn't recognize having ever picked 
up: a trans tunnel 73 orator relay, a Valte personal shield platinum edition, and a mag 340 Valte corp pulse pistol. All Valte corporation tech, all flagged illegal by the system AI. He realizes immediately these must 
have been in Agatha's cart during the ball of swine boss fight when he briefly added the entire thing to his inventory. Then Donut spots Agatha walking along. She's still alive, still level four, still pushing her 
squeaking shopping cart. She shuffles in, calls Carl a cart thief, and heads down the stairs. Then after they descend down the stairwell, the post-floor taping with Odette goes well. Deathwatch Extreme Dungeon Mayhem 
announces it's going on hiatus. King Rust of the Skull Empire has reportedly requested people stop calling the Maestro Carl's naughty little piggy, and we learn something happened between Odette and Mordecai back when 
she was his trainer. She pushed him too hard, and he paid for it, and she wants a chance to explain herself to him. She also tells Carl and Donut about a Pokéball equivalent which exists that will let them store 
[ __ ] in their inventory. Carl asks about the Valte corporation. Odette tells him they're a parasitic corporate life form. Carl then thinks of Agatha, though says nothing. Then Odette tells him [ __ ] is actually 
of a velociraptor. Her last piece of advice is that there might be something hidden in Donut's class choices only available to high charisma crawlers that will greatly increase their survival odds, though she doesn't 
know if it's morally right to take it. But if they do, it's going to make somebody very angry. And that is the end of book one of Dungeon Crawler Carl. Let me know what you think. Post in the comments if you find this 
video helpful, and I will definitely be looking into finishing with the rest of the books. Thank you very much. I'll see you next time.